# ⚠⚠ THIS FILE EXISTS TO PROTECT THE AUTHORED-SHA256 CONTRACT FROM GIT ITSELF.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on this machine. On CHECKOUT git rewrites LF -> CRLF for anything it
# considers text — so an artifact committed byte-for-byte would come back out of a fresh clone
# with different bytes, and its authored `sha256` would no longer match.
#
# ⚠ The failure would be silent and it would look like tampering: the hash mismatch is exactly the
# signal the contract uses to say "the channel altered this", and git would be producing it on a
# file nothing had touched. **A guard that cannot distinguish corruption from its own toolchain is
# not a guard.**
#
# Measured 2026-08-19: no `.gitattributes` existed, so nothing protected any file from the
# conversion. The artifacts below are delivered as FILES, hashed by their author over a declared
# range, and committed unmodified — see `docs/SPEC-2026-08-19-landed-artifact-provenance.md`.
#
# `-text` disables all end-of-line conversion in both directions. It is deliberately scoped to the
# hash-verified artifacts rather than applied to `docs/**`: a blanket rule would change how ~100
# existing documents round-trip, which is a different decision and not this one.

